  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 240 PM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThe strong high pressure over the southern U.S. will consolidate\nover the lower Mississippi Valley and northern Gulf most of next \nweek. This will result in a generally W-NW low/mid level wind flow\nacross South Florida through at least Wednesday. In August, a \nnortherly component to the wind usually means well above normal \ntemperatures, and the upcoming work week is primed to be the\nhottest period so far this summer. The unseasonably high\ntemperatures in the low levels will extend through the 700 mb\nlevel as the high pressure expands, and further increase our\nconfidence in impactful heat. Continued to lean on the 75th\npercentile NBM max temperatures for Monday and Tuesday which\nyields upper 90s over most of South Florida, including east coast\nmetro areas very close to the coast. We even have a few 100-degree\nspots in the forecast over the Everglades Monday and Tuesday, and\none or two spots over the outlying suburbs could also reach 100F.\nAlthough some afternoon mixing of drier air to the surface may \nlower dewpoints to the lower 70s, heat index values are still\nexpected to top out close to (if not exceed) 110F. The GFS\nensemble probability of heat index above 110F in Miami is between\n35-40% Tuesday and Wednesday. Therefore, not only are Heat\nAdvisories an almost sure bet, but we'll have to consider Extreme\nHeat Warnings (110F or higher heat index in Miami-Dade/Broward and\n113F or higher elsewhere) if trends continue. As expected,\nHeatRisk is showing 20-40% chances of Extreme (level 4 of 4) over\nparts of metro SE Florida. Only slight relief is possible for the \nend of the week, but still remaining above normal.\n\nPWAT values are forecast to remain in the lowest 5% of climatology\non Monday (1.3 to 1.6 inches), and slowly increase to around 1.7\nto 1.9 inches Thursday and Friday. As a result, PoPs stay below\nAugust normals for most of the week, no higher than 20-30% each\nafternoon.",
